ntry who  think that they can with impunity punish  speech they don't like the law is  incredibly clear they can't what is what  is it about these university  administrators they seem to be among the  most spineless people and harm is a  self-selection thing where they're  attracted to this line of business or do  they are they created like once they  enter that what would be the horrible  repercussions for them if they actually  did stand up for free speech well what's  going on with administrators is a puzzle  and it's very much a mixed bag I I  completely have met  administrators who are actually good on  these issues and I think the most of the  administrators are actually just not  being active enough to defend student  rights but then you have the one of the  one of the kinds of problem at  administrators you have are the  Crusaders the the ones that actually  think that it's their job as for example  at University of Delaware as residents  life to save the souls and psyches of  their students all 7,000 students were  subjected to what they what the resident  life called a treatment that was this  completely comprehensive indoctrination  program that included floor meetings in  which you had to stand on one side of  the room if you had one opinion on a  political issue on one side if you had  on the other basically being a public  shaming of students for the wrong points  of view and even creepier where you had  one on one mandatory one-on-one meetings  with your RA to discuss what races and  sexes you would date a female student  who objected to this actually who said  none of your damn business she was  written up refer for a potential  punishment what is the big picture in  all this how many roughly how many  schools have speech codes how bad is it  in broad terms this is actually good  news of the nearly three hundred schools  that we rate only 71% of them have what  we call red light speech codes  that's laughably unconstitutional or  would be unconstitutional at a public  college that's good news because in  previous years and the ratings all done  by the same person we've gotten as high  as 79 percent all of this all of these  speech codes are somewhat perversely  done in the name of education that  teaching students how to be civil and  teaching students how to how to be  respectful intolerant the but what  you're missing students about is is  about what their rights actually are and  when these speech coats are everywhere  you start talking to students and they  just assume they can't have a  controversial opinion so they just avoid  talking about controversial things in a  lot of cases students are internalizing  the values of censorship as actually  things that good people do that and I  find that personally terrifying and and  if you really understand what's at stake  when you when you limit freedom of  speech that when you understand that our  entire intellectual system going far  beyond just the scientific method  depends on open exchange of ideas and  and and thought experiments then you you  go there ready to battle but not just  the abuses of law but the but the the  weirdly warp mentality that puts  censorship on the side of the angels and  free speech on the side of the devil's  it's completely reversed  are there any schools that consistently  over the years defend free expression um  you know it's funny I I usually would  say Penn State's been pretty good about  a Graham Spanier has been pretty good at  Penn State but we're just releasing a  video right now about a case where there  was a student who is making art a Jewish  student was making art that was critical  of radical Islam and talking about  Palestinian terrorism against Israelis  and his his art was was banned because a  professor claimed it didn't foster  democratic dialogue and that it violated  the university code it's it's amazing  how mental gymnastics people have to do  to justify censorship  I was happy though that Graham Spanier  be